  <h1>Harness<span class="ink-orange">.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The harness is how skipr says <em>no</em>. A short <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in your project folder,
    plus scripts under <code>checks/</code> that must be allowed to fail. Fewer skills, more checks.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#what">What it installs</a>
    <a href="#how">How to run it</a>
    <a href="#source">Skill source on GitHub</a>
    <a href="#after">After install</a>
    <a href="/docs">← Back to Get started</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="what">What setup-harness does</h2>
  <p>When you run the skill in a creative/project folder, it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Writes a short <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (body aimed at ≤ 40 lines)</li>
    <li>Creates <code>checks/</code> with at least one executable script that exits non-zero on failure</li>
    <li>Optionally adds at most two <b>local</b> skills under <code>.claude/skills/</code></li>
    <li>Never copies Spec Kit, a Second Brain, or a pile of global skills into the project</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Domains it knows (pick one): trip / client / culling / delivery — each maps to a default checker.</p>

  <h2 id="how">How to run it (beginner)</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Install the skill on your machine</h3>
      <p>
        Copy <code>skills/setup-harness/</code> from the skipr repo into
        <code>~/.claude/skills/setup-harness/</code> so Claude Code can load it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3><code>cd</code> into the project folder</h3>
      <p>The harness is folder-scoped. Do not run it from a generic prompt vault.</p>
      <pre class="cmd">cd ~/Projects/your-folder
claude</pre>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Ask Claude to run the skill</h3>
      <pre class="cmd">Run setup-harness for this folder
# or in Spanish: Monta el harness / arnés en esta carpeta</pre>
      <p>Answer the one domain question if it asks. Then let it write files and chmod the checks.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Smoke-test a check</h3>
      <pre class="cmd">./checks/naming.sh</pre>
      <p>Pass or fail with a clear message — both are useful. See <code>checks/README.md</code>.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

